speechtext:
That the lessee shall supply electric power to the satisfaction of the chief engineer of railways and canals for the working of all the locks on the Cornwall canal at sixty-three dollars per horse-power per annum ;
That the lessee shall also supply and work electric direct current arc lights of not less than 2,000 candle power, in perfect working order, at such points and such manner as shall,, from time to time be deemed necessary, by the chief engineer aforesaid, running all night whenever so required, along the caDal, &c.
Then the lessee , was obliged to place such poles as should be directed for the purpose of conveying this power to the lights anti I suppose also for the purpose of conveying power to the stations at which it was to be received for the operation of the canal. Up to that point you have not very much more than this ; that the government have the right to name the number of lights and they have the right to fix a certain number of horse-power which are to be supplied by the lessee. But until the government fix the number of lights and horse-power which they require, the lessee has not very much comfort out of the contract so far as anything is concerned that he might receive out of it. Up to that time it is an option on the part of the government, with the qualification that when they do exercise that option, they shall pay for the light and the power supplied at certain fixed rates. Does that idea agree with the view of the Minister of Justice ?
